In-memory store: activity = max(createdAt (Date.now()), entry timestamps). So to seed three conversations with distinct activity, I create sessions with entries carrying explicit timestamps in the past/future relative to Date.now(). Since createdAt = Date.now() (today, 2026-10-31), seeding entry timestamps of e.g. '2026-01-01' would be OLDER than createdAt. So newest-first ordering via entries won't work unless I use future timestamps, or control creation order.

Simplest robust seeding: create the three conversations in the desired order (creation times ascending), each with entries carrying an ISO timestamp of its own. But createdAt is Date.now() for all three, nearly identical ms — sorting by activity where activity = max(createdAt, entry stamps)... If I give entries timestamps in the FUTURE (e.g. '2027-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', '2027-01-02...', '2027-01-03...'), activity is unambiguous regardless of creation ms. That's clean: three sessions for 'u1', each with one user entry stamped at distinct future times. And one session for 'u2' with its own stamp, for the authorisation test.

Hmm, wait: entryCount = entries.length; I want summaries distinguishable. Good.

Note: `createdAt` in summaries is `Date.now()` at creation — an exact ms value. On the wire it becomes an ISO string with '.000Z' — so `new Date(x).toISOString() === x` round-trip holds for both createdAt and updatedAt.

